1
00:00:04,800 --> 00:00:10,124
You meant to start a feature branch, but committed two commits directly on main.

2
00:00:10,667 --> 00:00:15,637
Which branch am I on, and which commits are not supposed to be on it?

3
00:00:15,637 --> 00:00:22,625
Compare with the last known-good point (here the shared main ends at "Add prices"; with a remote,

4
00:00:22,625 --> 00:00:23,561
origin/main):

5
00:00:24,100 --> 00:00:27,805
git switch -c feature was forgotten before committing.

6
00:00:27,805 --> 00:00:31,030
Commits always go to the branch HEAD points to.

7
00:00:32,767 --> 00:00:38,306
Create the branch where main is now (it keeps the commits), then move main back:

8
00:00:38,867 --> 00:00:46,171
A shell prompt that shows the branch (Git's git-prompt.sh, starship, oh-my-zsh).

9
00:00:46,733 --> 00:00:51,948
Protect main on the server so direct pushes fail (lesson 59).

10
00:00:53,700 --> 00:00:57,570
Merging a feature branch into main stops with a conflict.

11
00:00:58,133 --> 00:01:01,578
Who changed the line on each side, and why?

12
00:01:02,133 --> 00:01:05,879
Two branches changed the same lines in parallel.

13
00:01:05,879 --> 00:01:07,440
Not an error in Git:

14
00:01:07,440 --> 00:01:10,093
a decision only a person can make.

15
00:01:11,833 --> 00:01:16,966
Decide the correct content (here, after asking both:

16
00:01:16,966 --> 00:01:21,803
3.40), remove the markers, mark resolved, commit:

17
00:01:22,367 --> 00:01:29,256
Small, short-lived branches; merge main into your branch often (lesson 56).

18
00:01:29,800 --> 00:01:35,965
git config --global merge.conflictStyle zdiff3 to see the base when conflicts happen.

19
00:01:37,700 --> 00:01:38,421
git add .

20
00:01:38,421 --> 00:01:44,033
and git commit included a file that should never be in the repository:

21
00:01:44,033 --> 00:01:47,560
a 200 kB debug log, next to the real change.

22
00:01:48,100 --> 00:01:50,095
Is the commit pushed?

23
00:01:50,095 --> 00:01:52,185
What exactly is in it?

24
00:01:52,733 --> 00:01:53,534
git add .

25
00:01:53,534 --> 00:01:58,693
stages everything untracked; the log file was not ignored.

26
00:02:00,433 --> 00:02:06,613
Take the file out of the commit, keep the real change, and ignore such files from now on:

27
00:02:07,167 --> 00:02:12,361
Read git status (or git diff --cached --stat) before every commit.

28
00:02:12,900 --> 00:02:17,855
Add files by name or with git add -p; keep a good .gitignore.

29
00:02:19,600 --> 00:02:23,924
Meaning to drop one commit, you ran git reset --hard HEAD~3:

30
00:02:23,924 --> 00:02:26,735
three commits are gone from the branch.

31
00:02:27,300 --> 00:02:30,840
The reflog records where HEAD was before the reset:

32
00:02:31,400 --> 00:02:34,270
--hard with a larger count than intended.

33
00:02:34,270 --> 00:02:39,380
reset moves the branch; it does not remove commits from the object store.

34
00:02:41,133 --> 00:02:46,584
If other commands ran since (so ORIG_HEAD moved), use the reflog entry:

35
00:02:46,584 --> 00:02:48,580
git reset --hard HEAD@{N}.

36
00:02:48,580 --> 00:02:52,879
Uncommitted changes lost by --hard are a different case:

37
00:02:52,879 --> 00:02:54,798
Problem 16 and lesson 81.

38
00:02:55,333 --> 00:03:01,948
Prefer git reset --soft / --mixed (keep the changes) and git revert for shared commits.

39
00:03:02,500 --> 00:03:05,539
Before risky commands, mark the spot:

40
00:03:05,539 --> 00:03:08,249
git branch backup-before-cleanup.

41
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:14,849
Cleaning up branches, you force-deleted one that was not merged yet.

42
00:03:15,400 --> 00:03:18,771
The deletion message printed the tip (was …).

43
00:03:18,771 --> 00:03:23,339
If it scrolled away, the reflog has the branch's last commit:

44
00:03:23,900 --> 00:03:29,162
git branch -D deletes even unmerged branches (-d would have refused).

45
00:03:29,162 --> 00:03:32,364
A branch is a pointer; the commits remain.

46
00:03:35,600 --> 00:03:42,039
Delete with git branch -d (refuses unmerged branches); use -D only after checking.

47
00:03:42,600 --> 00:03:44,929
Push work-in-progress branches:

48
00:03:44,929 --> 00:03:47,709
the server and teammates keep a copy.

49
00:03:49,467 --> 00:03:56,258
You checked out a release tag to look at it, fixed something, committed, and switched back.

50
00:03:56,258 --> 00:03:57,676
The fix is nowhere.

51
00:03:58,233 --> 00:04:00,659
Was HEAD detached when committing?

52
00:04:00,659 --> 00:04:06,793
The reflog shows the checkout of a commit ID (not a branch) and the commit made there:

53
00:04:07,333 --> 00:04:15,873
Commits made in detached HEAD belong to no branch; switching away leaves them unreferenced (Git printed a warning with the commit ID).

54
00:04:17,633 --> 00:04:21,328
Give it a branch, then bring it where it belongs:

55
00:04:21,867 --> 00:04:27,522
When Git says "You are in 'detached HEAD' state", create a branch before committing:

56
00:04:27,522 --> 00:04:28,801
git switch -c NAME.

57
00:04:29,367 --> 00:04:36,841
Inspect old versions with git switch --detach TAG consciously, or use a worktree (lesson 87).

58
00:04:38,600 --> 00:04:46,006
You copied a project folder to start a new service, but origin still points to the old project's repository.

59
00:04:46,006 --> 00:04:49,230
Your first pull brings in a stranger's history.

60
00:04:49,767 --> 00:04:53,226
Where does origin point, and what is there?

61
00:04:53,767 --> 00:04:58,996
The remote URL was copied with the folder (or added from the wrong snippet).

62
00:05:00,733 --> 00:05:04,393
Point origin at the right repository and push:

63
00:05:04,933 --> 00:05:09,998
After cloning or copying, run git remote -v before the first push.

64
00:05:10,533 --> 00:05:18,273
Start new projects with git init (or from a template repository), not by copying another project's .git.

65
00:05:20,033 --> 00:05:24,003
Your push to main is rejected right after a teammate pushed.

66
00:05:24,567 --> 00:05:28,976
What does the server have that you do not, and the other way round?

67
00:05:29,533 --> 00:05:33,849
Someone pushed to the same branch since your last pull.

68
00:05:33,849 --> 00:05:36,203
Normal teamwork, not an error.

69
00:05:37,967 --> 00:05:40,596
Integrate their work, then push.

70
00:05:40,596 --> 00:05:45,361
Rebase keeps the history linear (your commit goes on top):

71
00:05:45,900 --> 00:05:53,010
git pull --rebase (or fetch + look) before pushing; pull.rebase true as default.

72
00:05:53,567 --> 00:05:56,383
Work on branches and merge through PRs:

73
00:05:56,383 --> 00:05:59,561
then two people rarely push the same branch.

74
00:06:01,300 --> 00:06:07,955
You cleaned up your already-pushed feature branch (amended the last commit) and now cannot push it.

75
00:06:08,500 --> 00:06:11,475
Compare your branch with its pushed version:

76
00:06:12,033 --> 00:06:18,088
History was rewritten (amend, rebase, reset) after pushing.

77
00:06:19,833 --> 00:06:21,647
Decide whose branch it is:

78
00:06:21,647 --> 00:06:25,065
Only you use it (a feature branch before review):

79
00:06:25,065 --> 00:06:27,506
replace the remote version, safely.

80
00:06:27,506 --> 00:06:29,041
Others have pulled it:

81
00:06:29,041 --> 00:06:34,063
do not rewrite; restore the pushed version and add a new commit instead.

82
00:06:34,600 --> 00:06:40,245
Rewrite history only before pushing, or only on branches nobody else uses.

83
00:06:40,800 --> 00:06:48,285
Use --force-with-lease, never plain --force; protect main against force pushes (lesson 59).

84
00:06:50,033 --> 00:06:52,556
A freshly set-up laptop:

85
00:06:52,556 --> 00:06:57,813
git push to GitHub fails, over SSH and over HTTPS.

86
00:06:58,367 --> 00:07:02,799
Is it the network, the host key, or the key?

87
00:07:02,799 --> 00:07:05,921
Ask SSH directly, with details:

88
00:07:06,467 --> 00:07:10,494
Credentials were never set up on this machine:

89
00:07:10,494 --> 00:07:15,221
no SSH key registered with GitHub, no token for HTTPS.

90
00:07:16,967 --> 00:07:20,099
Either protocol works; pick one.

91
00:07:20,099 --> 00:07:20,490
SSH:

92
00:07:20,490 --> 00:07:30,376
create a key and register its public part on GitHub (Settings → SSH and GPG keys, or gh ssh-key add):

93
00:07:30,933 --> 00:07:34,089
One documented setup per laptop:

94
00:07:34,089 --> 00:07:41,388
gh auth login (HTTPS) or an SSH key with a passphrase, added to the agent.

95
00:07:41,933 --> 00:07:44,817
In CI, never personal credentials:

96
00:07:44,817 --> 00:07:49,228
GITHUB_TOKEN, deploy keys or short-lived app tokens.

97
00:07:50,967 --> 00:07:54,380
You created a fix branch from origin/main.

98
00:07:54,380 --> 00:08:00,556
git status compares it with main, git pull pulls main, and git push refuses.

99
00:08:03,100 --> 00:08:08,000
The branch was created from a remote-tracking branch, so it inherited it as upstream.

100
00:08:10,733 --> 00:08:14,683
Push the branch under its own name and make that the upstream:

101
00:08:15,233 --> 00:08:22,818
Create branches from the local main (git switch main && git pull && git switch -c NAME), or use.

102
00:08:23,367 --> 00:08:26,979
git config --global push.autoSetupRemote true:

103
00:08:26,979 --> 00:08:30,906
the first git push creates and tracks origin/NAME.

104
00:08:32,667 --> 00:08:37,319
A database password went into a commit, and the commit was pushed.

105
00:08:37,319 --> 00:08:39,786
(All values in this lab are fake.).

106
00:08:40,333 --> 00:08:45,188
Which commits contain it, is it pushed, and where else could it be?

107
00:08:45,733 --> 00:08:51,870
A configuration file with a real credential was committed; nothing (.gitignore,

108
00:08:51,870 --> 00:08:55,598
pre-commit scanner, push protection) stopped it.

109
00:08:57,333 --> 00:08:57,475
1.

110
00:08:57,475 --> 00:09:02,513
Rotate the credential now (change the database password at the source).

111
00:09:03,067 --> 00:09:10,176
.gitignore for configuration with secrets; commit templates (config.example.yml).

112
00:09:10,733 --> 00:09:16,538
A pre-commit secret scanner (lesson 83) and GitHub push protection.

113
00:09:18,300 --> 00:09:27,610
A push is rejected because one commit contains a file over the server's size limit.

114
00:09:27,610 --> 00:09:32,995
Deleting the file in a new commit does not help.

115
00:09:33,533 --> 00:09:38,063
Which unpushed commits are there, and which contains the big file?

116
00:09:38,600 --> 00:09:41,748
A large binary was committed into history.

117
00:09:41,748 --> 00:09:46,845
The server checks every pushed object, not only the latest snapshot.

118
00:09:48,600 --> 00:09:51,915
The commits are not pushed, so rewrite them:

119
00:09:51,915 --> 00:10:00,580
drop the file from "Add menu photos" with an interactive rebase (or move it to Git LFS with git lfs migrate import,

120
00:10:00,580 --> 00:10:01,409
lesson 88).

121
00:10:01,409 --> 00:10:04,725
Here the photos do not belong in Git at all:

122
00:10:05,267 --> 00:10:12,511
.gitignore for archives, media and build output; store them in object storage or a release asset.

123
00:10:13,067 --> 00:10:19,276
Git LFS for large files that must be versioned (git lfs track before adding them).

124
00:10:21,033 --> 00:10:29,773
git pull --rebase (or git rebase main) stops halfway; the prompt says REBASE 1/2 and nothing seems to work.

125
00:10:32,333 --> 00:10:37,637
Your branch and main changed the same line; rebase applies your commits one by one,

126
00:10:37,637 --> 00:10:41,088
so the conflict appears at the commit that touched it.

127
00:10:42,833 --> 00:10:49,058
Resolve this commit, mark it, continue; repeat if another commit conflicts:

128
00:10:49,600 --> 00:10:54,649
Rebase often (small steps) rather than once after weeks.

129
00:10:55,200 --> 00:11:03,195
git config --global rerere.enabled true to reuse resolutions; merge.conflictStyle zdiff3 for context.

130
00:11:04,933 --> 00:11:08,608
An unfinished branch was merged into main and pushed.

131
00:11:08,608 --> 00:11:12,698
It must come out of main, without rewriting shared history.

132
00:11:13,233 --> 00:11:15,903
Find the merge and what it brought in:

133
00:11:16,467 --> 00:11:20,192
The wrong branch was merged (or merged too early).

134
00:11:20,192 --> 00:11:24,066
A PR review or protected main would have stopped it.

135
00:11:25,800 --> 00:11:31,590
Revert the merge, keeping the first parent (main's side), as a new commit:

136
00:11:32,133 --> 00:11:38,753
Merge only through reviewed PRs into a protected main (lessons 52–59).

137
00:11:39,300 --> 00:11:46,325
Name unfinished work clearly (wip/…, draft PRs) and keep it out of the merge queue.

138
00:11:48,067 --> 00:11:53,946
During a git pull --rebase with a conflict, you ran git rebase --skip to "get past it".

139
00:11:53,946 --> 00:11:56,446
Later you notice your commit is gone.

140
00:11:57,000 --> 00:12:01,855
The commit was never pushed, so only this clone's reflog knows it:

141
00:12:02,400 --> 00:12:07,274
git rebase --skip means "drop this commit", not "skip the conflict".

142
00:12:09,033 --> 00:12:13,575
Re-apply the lost commit; it conflicts again on the latte line,

143
00:12:13,575 --> 00:12:18,478
this time resolve it properly (keep chai, agree on the latte price):

144
00:12:19,033 --> 00:12:24,913
In a conflict, resolve or --abort; use --skip only when you want the commit gone.

145
00:12:25,467 --> 00:12:26,534
Small commits:

146
00:12:26,534 --> 00:12:31,411
a conflict in one change does not put unrelated changes at risk.

147
00:12:33,167 --> 00:12:36,719
A wrong commit sits in the middle of the history:

148
00:12:36,719 --> 00:12:37,226
"Debug:

149
00:12:37,226 --> 00:12:41,069
log all orders", which writes customer data to a log.

150
00:12:41,069 --> 00:12:44,476
It must go, but the commits after it must stay.

151
00:12:45,033 --> 00:12:46,347
Is it pushed?

152
00:12:46,347 --> 00:12:49,178
What exactly does it change?

153
00:12:49,733 --> 00:12:54,713
A debugging change was committed together with real work and pushed without review.

154
00:12:56,467 --> 00:12:59,548
Choose by whether the commit is shared:

155
00:12:59,548 --> 00:13:01,760
Pushed / shared (this case):

156
00:13:01,760 --> 00:13:02,866
git revert it:

157
00:13:02,866 --> 00:13:07,922
a new commit that removes exactly its change, safe for everyone.

158
00:13:07,922 --> 00:13:08,791
Only local:

159
00:13:08,791 --> 00:13:13,531
drop it with git rebase -i (lesson 63), so it never existed.

160
00:13:14,067 --> 00:13:21,446
Review git diff --cached before committing; keep debugging changes on a separate branch or in .gitignored files.

161
00:13:22,000 --> 00:13:25,779
Pull requests with review before anything reaches main.

162
00:13:27,533 --> 00:13:30,547
The team deploys from a production branch.

163
00:13:30,547 --> 00:13:37,721
A commit meant for the next release (a new price list) was cherry-picked into production by mistake,

164
00:13:37,721 --> 00:13:39,228
pushed, and deployed.

165
00:13:39,767 --> 00:13:42,564
What is on production that should not be?

166
00:13:42,564 --> 00:13:45,771
Compare it with the last release and with main:

167
00:13:46,333 --> 00:13:50,217
Too much was cherry-picked into the production branch,

168
00:13:50,217 --> 00:13:54,533
and nothing (review, protection) checked it before the push.

169
00:13:56,267 --> 00:13:58,795
Production is shared and deployed:

170
00:13:58,795 --> 00:14:05,710
revert the unwanted commit on production (never reset a deployed branch), push, and redeploy.

171
00:14:05,710 --> 00:14:10,171
main is not touched, so the price list still ships in March:

172
00:14:10,733 --> 00:14:13,915
Protect production (and release branches):

173
00:14:13,915 --> 00:14:18,158
changes only via reviewed PRs, and tag every deployment.

174
00:14:18,700 --> 00:14:21,264
Release from tags (Module 21):

175
00:14:21,264 --> 00:14:28,014
a deployment is "tag vX.Y.Z", and rolling back is redeploying the previous tag.
